  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef is shopping for ingredients for his restaurant, and has with him a **single** discount coupon.\n\nThere are NNN ingredients Chef would like to buy. The iii-th of them has a price of AiA_iAi​, but Chef can buy it at a price of BiB_iBi​ if he uses the discount coupon on it.\n\nThe store is running a special campaign: every customer is given several entries into a raffle — in particular, a customer receives a number of entries equal to the greatest common divisor of the prices of all the items they bought.\n\nFor example, if the prices of the items are [9,18,33][9, 18, 33][9,18,33] then Chef would receive gcd⁡(9,18,33)=3\\gcd(9, 18, 33) = 3gcd(9,18,33)=3 entries to the raffle.\n\nChef is only interested in maximizing his winning chances in the raffle by obtaining as many entries as possible.\n\nIf he can use his discount coupon on **at most one item**, what's the maximum number of entries he can obtain?\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of three lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single space-separated integer NNN — the number of ingredients Chef would like to buy.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​ — the initial prices of the ingredients.\nThe third line contains NNN space-separated integers B1,B2,…,BNB_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_NB1​,B2​,…,BN​ — the discounted prices of the ingredients.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer — the maximum number of raffle entries Chef can obtain, if he uses his discount coupon optimally.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤Bi≤Ai≤1091 \\leq B_i \\leq A_i \\leq 10^91≤Bi​≤Ai​≤109",
      "The sum of NNN across all tests won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3\n24 51 96\n9 44 68\n2\n20 17\n17 10\n4\n10 20 30 40\n5 15 25 35",
        "output": "4\n17\n10",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: It's optimal to use the discount on the second item. This makes the prices of the items [24,44,96][24, 44, 96][24,44,96], and gcd⁡(24,44,96)=4\\gcd(24, 44, 96) = 4gcd(24,44,96)=4. There is no way to obtain a larger GCD than 444.\nTest case 222: It's optimal to use the discount coupon on the first item, making the prices [17,17][17, 17][17,17] for a GCD of 171717.\nTest case 333: It's optimal to not use the discount coupon at all, to attain a GCD of 101010."
      }
    ]
  },
